.TH DF 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
df \- report the size and free space of drives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
.RB [ \-PkmhHTaw ]
.RB [ \-B
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR type ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B df
prints the size, used space, and available space of drives on standard
output.
It prints a header line, then a line for each drive it reports.
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, it reports each drive letter, in alphabetical order, whose
drive is fixed, removable, CD-ROM, or network and whose volume
information can be read.
A drive of another type, such as a RAM disk, and a drive that cannot be
read, such as one with no disk in it, are left out.
Then it reports each folder where a volume is mounted, other than the
root of a drive, taking the volumes in the order Windows lists them.
A volume mounted in several folders gets a line for each.
If the volume information of such a folder cannot be read, the list ends
there.
Any of these whose size is 0 is left out, unless
.B \-a
is given.
.PP
With operands,
.B df
reports one line for each
.IR file ,
in order: the first drive or folder in the list above whose volume
holds
.IR file .
A drive name such as
.B D:
selects that drive.
Otherwise volumes are told apart by serial number, so a path through a
junction, symbolic link, or volume mount point selects the volume it
leads to, and a UNC path such as
.I //server/share/dir
selects a network drive mapped to the same volume of that server.
A file on a volume that is not in the list, such as one on a RAM disk or
on a network share with no drive letter, is an error.
So, normally, is a file whose serial number busybox cannot read, such as
a cloud file not yet downloaded.
A drive or folder selected this way is reported even when its size is 0.
.SS Output format
The header names the fields of each line:
.TP
.B Filesystem
The drive, such as
.BR C: ,
or, when the root of the drive resolves to another path, as for a
network drive, that path, as in
.IR //server/share .
A folder shows the drive of the volume mounted there, or, when that
volume has no drive letter, its volume name, as in
\fB\e\e?\eVolume{\fIguid\fB}\e\fR.
With
.BR \-w ,
every volume that has a volume name shows it.
A network drive has none.
.TP
.B Type
Only with
.BR \-T :
the name Windows gives the file system, such as
.BR NTFS ,
.BR FAT32 ,
or
.BR exFAT .
.TP
.B 1K\-blocks
The size of the drive.
Its header is the unit, so it varies as described below, and with
.B \-h
or
.B \-H
it is
.BR Size .
.TP
.B Used
The size minus the free space.
.TP
.B Available
The free space available to the current user.
It can be less than the free space of the drive when disk quotas apply.
.TP
.B Use%
Used as a percentage of Used plus Available, rounded to the nearest
whole number.
With
.B \-P
the header is
.BR Capacity .
.TP
.B Mounted on
The root directory of the drive, such as
.IR C:/ ,
or the folder, such as
.IR C:/mnt/data ,
with no slash at the end.
.PP
Sizes are numbers of blocks of 1024 bytes, each rounded to the nearest
whole number, unless
.BR \-B ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-m ,
or
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
selects another unit.
Without
.B \-h
or
.BR \-H ,
the size header gives the block size as a plain number when it is under
1024, as in
.BR 512\-blocks .
Otherwise it is a whole number of K, M, or G, each 1024 times the last,
so that
.B \-B 1500
is headed
.BR 2K\-blocks .
With
.B \-P
it is always a plain number, as in
.BR 1500\-blocks .
.PP
A space separates the fields.
Filesystem is padded to 20 columns, Type to 10, and numbers are
right-aligned in 9, or in 3 for the percentage, which is followed by a
percent sign.
A number wider than 9 columns shifts the fields after it.
A Filesystem value wider than 20 columns, or a Type value wider than 10,
is followed by a line break, and the remaining fields continue on the
next line in their usual columns.
With
.B \-P
such a value stays on the line, followed by one extra space for each
column over the limit.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-hT ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-B1M ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Of
.BR \-B ,
.BR \-k ,
and
.BR \-m ,
the last one given applies, and
.B \-h
or
.B \-H
overrides all three.
.TP
.B \-P
POSIX output format: a block size in the size header is in bytes, the
percentage is headed
.BR Capacity ,
and long Filesystem and Type values do not wrap.
The unit does not change.
.TP
.B \-k
Use blocks of 1024 bytes.
This is the default, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.TP
.B \-m
Use blocks of 1048576 bytes.
.TP
.B \-h
Print sizes in human-readable form: a number with one decimal place and
a
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T ,
or larger suffix for a power of 1024, as in
.BR 932.7G .
A size of zero is printed as
.BR 0 .
.TP
.B \-H
The same as
.BR \-h .
Sizes still use powers of 1024.
.TP
.B \-T
Print the Type field.
.TP
.BI "\-t " type
Report only drives whose file system name, as printed by
.BR \-T ,
is exactly
.IR type ,
including case, such as
.BR NTFS .
This applies to operands too, and other drives are skipped without an
error.
Only the last
.B \-t
applies.
.TP
.B \-a
Also list drives and folders whose size is 0.
.TP
.BI "\-B " size
Use blocks of
.I size
bytes.
.I size
is a decimal number with an optional suffix, or a suffix alone, which
counts as 1 of that unit:
.BR K ,
.BR k ,
.BR KiB ,
or
.B kiB
for 1024;
.BR M ,
.BR m ,
.BR MiB ,
or
.B miB
for 1048576;
.BR G ,
.BR g ,
.BR GiB ,
or
.B giB
for 1073741824;
.B KB
for 1000;
.B MB
for 1000000; and
.B GB
for 1000000000.
The result must be from 1 to 4294967295, so
.B 4G
is too large.
An invalid
.I size
is an error even with
.B \-h
or
.BR \-H ,
but not when a later
.B \-k
or
.B \-m
overrides
.BR \-B .
.TP
.B \-w
In the Filesystem field, show the volume name of each volume that has
one, such as
\fB\e\e?\eVolume{\fIguid\fB}\e\fR,
in place of its drive or path.
The name is 49 characters long, so without
.B \-P
the rest of the line moves to the next.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows may show its critical-error dialog for a drive with no disk in
it.
Otherwise busybox suppresses the dialog, and
.B df
treats the drive as unreadable.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
console output is not converted to the console code page, and when the
ANSI code page is UTF-8, each non-ASCII byte in the Filesystem and Type
fields is replaced by a question mark.
Otherwise, with a UTF-8 code page, only invalid UTF-8 and nonprintable
characters are replaced, and a wide character counts as two columns when
padding.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the default block size is 512 bytes, and the first operand
ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, even when
.B \-t
leaves nothing to report.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, with a usage message on standard error; an invalid
.B \-B
size; an operand that does not exist or is on no volume in the list; or a
drive or folder whose space cannot be read.
The first two end
.B df
before it prints anything.
After the others the remaining operands, drives, and folders are still
reported.
.PP
An operand that does not exist or is on no volume in the list is
reported on standard error as the first line below, and a drive or
folder whose space cannot be read as the second, where
.I root
is the root of the drive, such as
.IR D:/ ,
or the folder with a slash at the end:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
df: \fIfile\fR: can\(aqt find mount point
df: \fIroot\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the space on every drive in human-readable form:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ df \-h
Filesystem                Size      Used Available Use% Mounted on
C:                      932.7G    718.6G    214.1G  77% C:/
D:                      466.1G    245.9G    220.2G  53% D:/
F:                       10.9T      6.3T      4.6T  58% F:/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the drive that holds the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ df .
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the space available on drive
.BR D: ,
in MiB, for use in a script.
The
.B \-P
option keeps a long Filesystem value on the same line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ df \-P \-m D: | awk \(aqNR == 2 { print $4 }\(aq
225460
.EE
.RE
.PP
List only the NTFS drives, with their types:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ df \-T \-t NTFS
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR df :
.IP \(bu 2
Network shares without a drive letter are not listed.
.IP \(bu 2
An operand on one of two volumes with the same serial number, such as a
disk and a copy of it, selects whichever is listed first.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B \-i
for inodes, no
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-x ,
or
.BR \-\-total ,
and no long options other than
.BR \-\-help .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-H
uses powers of 1024, not 1000.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-t
cannot be repeated to select several types.
.PP
Windows reports sizes in bytes, but not the block size.
.B df
assumes blocks of 4 KiB for a drive under 16 TiB, and 8, 16, 32, or
64 KiB for a drive under 32, 64, or 128 TiB, or larger.
The size, the free space, and the available space are each rounded down
to whole blocks of this size before conversion to the output unit, so
even
.B \-B 1
does not give exact byte counts.
.PP
Operands may use drive letters in either case and backslashes.
Lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR stat (1)
